            <div xml:id="d57">

                <pb facs="0188" n="92" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg92"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51 W 89France/296: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-05-05T13:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 5, 1926—1
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 5—10:45 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>173. It is alleged in press despatches that Congress intends to await French
                    Parliament’s ratification before any action is taken on the debt agreement. I
                    hope that this is not the case, since in my opinion prior ratification by the
                    American Congress will facilitate ratification in France. Ever since the time of
                    the Treaty of Versailles the French have been somewhat skeptical in regard to
                    action by the Senate, and opponents of the agreement could use as an argument
                    the possibility that it would not be ratified by the United States. French
                    ratification will not be easy to obtain, and everything possible to facilitate
                    the task of the French Government should be done.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
